libgweather (the C library)
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

This gem contains no code from libgweather and links nothing. It reads a
description of the library at runtime: libgweather ships a GObject
Introspection typelib, GWeather-4.0.typelib, and ruby-gnome's
GObjectIntrospection::Loader defines the GWeather namespace from it when the gem
is required. The library it describes belongs to the user's system and is not
redistributed here.

  https://gitlab.gnome.org/GNOME/libgweather
  Copyright 1999-2026 the GWeather authors
  Licensed under the GNU Lesser General Public License, version 2.1 or later

This is recorded rather than left to be discovered, because the licences differ:
libgweather is LGPL copyleft and this gem is Apache-2.0. The two coexist because
nothing is combined. There is no extension here, no static link and no vendored
source — at build time this gem does not know libgweather exists, and at runtime
it loads whichever copy the system provides, which is the separate
replaceability LGPL section 6 is about.

That remains true only while the arrangement does. Vendoring the library,
copying its headers, or adding an extension that links it are all different
licence questions, and none of them should be answered by doing it first.

The Locations.xml database — the world location tree that GWeather::Location
walks — is distributed with that C library under its terms, and is likewise not
redistributed by this gem.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
libgweather-rs
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

An earlier attempt at this gem was a hand-written Rust extension over
libgweather-rs, the gtk-rs binding. It was abandoned and removed: the typelib
describes the whole library for about twenty lines of loader, and the
cross-compile matrix could not build glib-sys anyway. No part of that work
survives in this product, and no code from libgweather-rs appears in it. The
reference is kept here so that the history is legible.

